A||||11||sprnt96||Dual exhaust for 4 cyl duke?||||1||
Z||000000||sprnt96||07-29-2001||06:17 PM||||I am planning on putting a dual exhaust on my 84SE 4 cyl as long as it is out being rebuilt and I was wondering the differences in performance and sound between a stock set up and a ocelot from the Fiero store?? being this is a 4 banger it may make no difference at all but i thought i would ask anyway||||reg||1
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||07-29-2001||07:08 PM||||I know that Jaygee79's 84SE sounds <B>a lot better</B> with duel exhaust. I used the exhaust off of a 86 GT. It looks nice from behind too.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000002||sprnt96||07-29-2001||07:30 PM||||What is the advantage to the Ocelot system compared to stock, anyone have any idea??||||reg||1
Z||000003||kslish||07-29-2001||09:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sprnt96:<BR><B>What is the advantage to the Ocelot system compared to stock, anyone have any idea??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Suppossedly a free-er flowing muffler....but the system is made out of aluminized steel piping and not stainless steel like stock.  So if you've already got a decent dual outlet stock system in good shape, I can't see a good reason to make the switch.  <P>However, if your making the switch to a Borla or something, than that is a different story   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'd give my input on how my 2.5L coupe sounds with a dual outlet exhaust, but I've put that project off until after the Williamsburg show.  I'm actually going to put a Borla system on my Duke just for the heck of it cause I've got a spare Borla system and I want the dual exhaust look on my coupe since I've got a Formula rear bumper on it that has both exhaust openings on it.  <P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Galen||07-29-2001||09:55 PM||||It could also be a 88 coupe bumper. Seeing as how 88 coupes had the dual cutouts as well<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"And God said there shall be one in thy owns image, and he made me"</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/download.html" TARGET=_blank>Download FieroProject.com Chat Client v1.50 Today</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||sprnt96||07-29-2001||10:09 PM||||I have changed the front and rear facia on this car, it now looks pretty much like an 86 SE with the ground effects and the wing.  It has an 85 GT front and rear facia so right now it looks odd with the single exhaust.  Only drove it once after doing all these modifications, burnt a piston 15 miles south of the dells in may...and not it sits waiting for the motor to be rebuit so i figure i might as well think of things to do to it as long as the motor is out||||reg||1
Z||000006||DandRauto||07-31-2001||02:48 PM||||If you don't have a good dual exhaust system, is the Ocelot the only choice for the duke if I don't want the the expense of the Borla.  Or can I just order a stock V6 dual exhaust system. Or what other choice do I need to get.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ChadMan||07-31-2001||03:22 PM||||Why not just buy a good second-hand V6 exhaust system from a junkyard, another Forum member, or a used-Fiero-parts seller like Ed Parks or Kick Hill Farm?  <P>You can use your existing Duke exhaust from the cat forward, but cut the pipe on the straight section that runs back from the cat to the muffler and splice the dual rear section in with a 2"ID connector pipe, available at any auto-parts store.<P>Or am I missing something?<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000008||Frank Sombers||07-31-2001||10:46 PM||||Sprnt96:<BR>I put duals on my '88 duke powered cpe. since the second exhaust cutout was already there.<BR>I went to a junk yard and got the muffler and the chrome dual tip tailpipes off an '86SE V6 for $25. I removed it (from the cat back) but that wasn't a problem. Next I had the muffler and tailpipes installed at a local muffler shop for $35.<BR>I understand that the V6 muffler is a higher flow than what is used on the 4. I kept the stock cat as I didn't think there would be much of an improvement in performance for the cost of a new free flow cat. (If I get the intake and exhaust manifolds port matched I might reconsider)<BR>By going this way I have a "correct" Fiero stainless steel exhaust system which makes the car look like a Formula.<BR>Is there a noticeable increase in performance? Honestly I don't think so, or very small if any.<BR>Does it sound better? Definitely.(not so much at idle, but surely when you're on the gas)<BR>Does it look better? Absolutely!!<BR>For only $60 total how can you go wrong!<BR>Save your money and search the junk yards.<BR>Have fun & Good luck! <BR>Frank<BR>  ||||reg||7
Z||000009||Dave Gunsul||08-01-2001||12:10 AM||||IRM sells a dual exaust that eliminates the <BR>muffler for both the 6 and 4cyl.<BR>This system is made of stainless too but is more expensive than a used V6 exhaust would<BR>obviously be.<BR>I don't remember what their web address is but you can call IRM at: (301)-948-3301<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
